sarah: quick check — packing the Node/TS SDK for edge runtimes (Next/Vercel Edge + Vite dev). Hitting multiple things at once: ESM vs CJS exports, bundler polyfills (undici/stream), auth token flow that used to read sync from disk, and streaming responses that look fine locally but get buffered/trimmed in prod behind Vite. anyone seen this combo? repro notes below.

mike: +1 we ran into this. recommended pattern: publish native ESM entrypoint and ship a transpiled CJS build under dist/cjs, then use conditional exports in package.json so bundlers pick the right one. keeps edge runtimes happy (pure ESM) and Node consumers get CJS if needed.

sarah: can you paste a minimal package.json snippet?

mike: sure — this is what worked for us:
```json
{
  "name": "redwood-inference",
  "version": "0.0.0",
  "main": "dist/cjs/index.js",
  "module": "dist/esm/index.js",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
      "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js"
    }
  },
  "type": "module"
}
```

anjali: auth note: on Edge you can't do sync fs or assume process.env at runtime the same way. treat envs as build-time bindings and move token retrieval into an async fetch inside the edge handler. keep refresh logic client-side (in the SDK) and use a small cache keyed by audience+scope to avoid frequent roundtrips. example pseudocode: 
```ts
// edge-safe token getter
async function getToken() {
  if (cache.valid) return cache.value
  const res = await fetch(TOKEN_URL, { method: 'POST', headers: { 'x-client-id': BINDING_CLIENT_ID } })
  const data = await res.json()
  cache.set(data.token, data.expires_in)
  return data.token
}
```
Don't call any Node-only libs (fs, crypto native wrappers) in that path. use Web Crypto APIs that exist on edge.

lee: streaming gotcha: Next Edge expects a Web ReadableStream and returning `new Response(stream)`; Node APIs like `res.write` won't work. Vite dev server sometimes transforms or polyfills streams which changes timing and causes small-chunk buffering. workaround: normalize to a Web ReadableStream at the SDK boundary and detect runtime. tiny helper: 
```ts
const isEdge = typeof Response !== 'undefined' && typeof process === 'undefined'
function toWebStream(asyncIterable) {
  return new ReadableStream({
    async pull(controller) {
      for await (const chunk of asyncIterable) controller.enqueue(chunk)
      controller.close()
    }
  })
}
// usage in edge: return new Response(toWebStream(generator()))
// usage in node server: pipe generator to res (if server adapter present)
```

mike: bundler tips: for Vite/Next dev, opt into ssr/noExternal for your SDK so vite doesn't try to pre-bundle Node-only deps. in vite.config.ts: 
```js
export default defineConfig({
  ssr: {
    noExternal: ['redwood-inference']
  }
})
```
And mark optional dependencies (like undici) as optionalPeer or conditional require behind runtime checks so the edge build doesn't pull them. e.g. `if (typeof fetch === 'undefined') { require('undici') }` — keep that inside runtime branches so bundlers can tree-shake.

anjali: also watch the `browser` / `exports` fields — some bundlers prioritize `browser` mappings and will swap in polyfills unexpectedly. if you need deterministic behavior, add a small runtime detection module that chooses the implementation explicitly instead of relying on bundler substitution.

sarah: great — one more: retries. our current retry middleware uses setTimeout and AbortController. any edge-specific caveats?

lee: AbortController + fetch is fine on modern edge runtimes but the timers behave differently in some providers. prefer using fetch with timeout signal and avoid long setTimeout chains in the edge handler (keep handlers < 50ms if you can). do retries on the SDK client side (outside the single edge request) when possible.

mike: for streaming+retry, you need to design idempotency and chunk-level checkpoints. if you can't resume a streaming response, fall back to non-streaming predictable path for retries.

